{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794393045,"nonce":"bfhaql4Er5rCfwOpyRltUiT+MsA=","title":"Drive library work with krio where a runtime has no threads to give it","message":"Assessment asked for by the user on 2026-11-11: how krio (~/Vibranium/krio, github.com/darmie/krio) could help HashLink and hxcpp.\n\nWhat hxn does today. `hxn_future::spawn` starts one OS thread per operation. On a wasm build without threads (an Ash `wasm32-wasip1` side module, hxcpp under Emscripten) it runs the work before returning, so the call blocks and nothing is ever pending.\n\nWhat krio offers that fits:\n\n- `krio_core::Task` and `Suspension` (`Completed`, `Yielded`, `Pending`) as the unit of library work, settled into a `Completer` when it completes.\n- `krio_runtime`'s `AgentRole::Main` and `drive_once`, which run one bounded pass and never park. That is the shape a host with one thread needs: the Haxe thread drives the library's tasks a slice at a time. `hxn.Future` already calls the library's `wait` repeatedly from `await` and `then`, so `wait` can be the pump and the Haxe API does not change.\n- `krio_parallel::Cluster` natively, as a pool with work stealing in place of a thread per operation, and `krio-wasm` for a wasm build with atomics.\n- A task stepped on the Haxe thread runs on a thread the VM knows. That is where a Haxe closure could be called from, which is the open half of the callbacks issue.\n\nWhat does not fit:\n\n- `krio-stackless` and `krio-async` are compiler transforms over a CFG, for a language implementation such as Ash. A Rust library author has no IR to hand them.\n- `krio-fiber` switches stacks on native targets only, and a `Fiber` is not `Send`. It adds nothing on wasm, which is where the gap is.\n\nWhat krio would need first:\n\n- A `Park` that blocks on a std host. Only `SpinPark` exists, and its own docs say it burns a core.\n- An adapter from a Rust `Future` to a `Task`, if authors are to write `async fn` as the scope document says. krio has no `core::task` integration today.\n\nCosts: `hxn-future` gains `krio-core`, `krio-runtime` and later `krio-parallel`. All three are `no_std` with no outside dependencies and type-check for `wasm32-wasip1` and `wasm32-unknown-emscripten`. krio is unpublished, so it would be a git dependency. Each library links its own scheduler, so several libraries in one program each get their own pool.\n\nLimits krio does not move: a closure passed to `spawn` is one step however it is scheduled, so work only becomes incremental if the author writes it as steps or as `async`; panics still abort on wasm; hxcpp's collector still hangs under Emscripten.\n\nSuggested order: the single-thread driver first (`krio-core` and `krio-runtime` behind `wait`), since it fixes the case that is wrong today; the native pool once a blocking `Park` exists.","files":null}]}